    "q-114458": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114458",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,3)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,3),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =9, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 4+{3}^{2}\\cdot 1+{4}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4+9\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =73.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3} & ∼N(9,73).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
