    "q-143105": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143105",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "A group of students' math test results are normally distributed with a mean of $70$ and a standard deviation of $6.$ Furthermore, their science test results are normally distributed with a mean of $65$ and a standard deviation of $10,$ while their language test results are normally distributed with a mean of $55$ and a standard deviation of $15.$ Find the probability that a randomly selected student's combined score is more than $200.$ You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the student's math, science, and language test results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(70,{6}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(65,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(55,{15}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total score of a randomly selected student. So, we have\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(70+65+55) \\\\  & =190, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={6}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{15}^{2} \\\\  & =36+100+225 \\\\  & =361.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(190,361).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>200),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & =P(Z>\\frac{200-190}{\\sqrt{361}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.53) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.53)≈0.7019.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & ≈1-Φ(0.53) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7019 \\\\  & =0.2981.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
