    "q-114463": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114463",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "A course grade is calculated by adding the results of a written test, oral test, and colloquium, contributing $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%,$ respectively to the final grade. \n\nThe written test results are normally distributed with a mean of $64$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ The oral test results are normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a standard deviation of $10$, while the results of the colloquium are normally distributed with a mean of $60$ and a standard deviation of $10.$ Find the probability that a randomly selected student achieves a grade greater than $70.$ You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the written test, oral test, and colloquium results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(64,{8}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(50,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(60,{10}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the final grade of a randomly selected student. Since the written, oral, and colloquium contribute $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%$, respectively, to the final grade, we have\n\n$$\nY=0.5{X}_{1}+0.3{X}_{2}+0.2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =0.5\\cdot 64+0.3\\cdot 50+0.2\\cdot 60 \\\\  & =59, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & =(0.5)^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+(0.3)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2}+(0.2)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot 64+0.09\\cdot 10+0.04\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ∼N(59,29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>70),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & =P(Z>\\frac{70-59}{\\sqrt{29}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.04)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.04)≈0.9793.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & ≈1-Φ(2.04) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9793 \\\\  & =0.0207.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
